Reason:
The `ServerSocket` is created without specifying a particular network interface, causing it to bind to all available interfaces. This can lead to unexpected behavior in tests, where subsequent attempts to bind to the same port are expected to fail, but may not do so due to the socket still being bound to other interfaces.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```java
InetAddress addr = InetAddress.getByName("localhost");
ServerSocket s = new ServerSocket(port, 50, addr);
```
```java
InetAddress addr = InetAddress.getByName("localhost");
ServerSocket s = new ServerSocket(port[0], 50, addr);
```
```java
InetAddress addr = InetAddress.getByName("localhost");
ServerSocket s = new ServerSocket(port[0], 50, addr);
```
Reason:
By explicitly binding the `ServerSocket` to the "localhost" interface, the code ensures that the socket is only bound to the loopback address. This prevents the issue of multiple bindings on different interfaces, ensuring that subsequent binding attempts on the same port will fail as expected in the tests.

